{
  "dom_id": "step-q85997",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Let $A={1,2,3}$ and consider the relation \"is equal to\" $(=)$ on $A.$ Which of the following sets describes the relation?",
  "worked_solution": "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x=y.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x=y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,1).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x=y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,2).$\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the value $y=3$ satisfies $x=y.$  So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(3,3).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(1,1),(2,2),(3,3)}\n$$",
